{"name":"Celtic Rush","hex":"#2e4c5b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sudden forward motion.","A surge, a sudden increase."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hurry; to perform a task with great haste, often not properly or without thinking carefully.","To flow or move forward rapidly or noisily."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rush (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Rush is a Canadian rock band, formed in Toronto in 1968. The band's original line-up included guitarist Alex Lifeson, drummer John Rutsey, and bassist and vocalist Jeff Jones, whom Geddy Lee replaced shortly after its formation. Rush went through a few line-up changes over the next six years, before settling on its classic power trio line-up with the addition of Neil Peart in July 1974, replacing Rutsey four months after the release of their self-titled debut album. The line-up of Lifeson, Lee, and Peart remained stable for the remainder of the band's initial run until 2015, after which Peart retired from music. Lifeson later confirmed in 2018 that the band had disbanded, citing Peart's health as a contributing factor. Lifeson and Lee continued to occasionally work together in the years following Peart's death in 2020. Lifeson and Lee announced a 2026–2027 reunion tour as Rush in October 2025, with Anika Nilles filling in for Peart, and Loren Gold as the band's keyboardist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Rush_band_1970s.jpg/330px-Rush_band_1970s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rush_(band)"},{"title":"Rush (1991 soundtrack)","blocks":[{"text":"Rush is the soundtrack album for the 1991 film of the same name. Written and performed by Eric Clapton, the soundtrack album includes the song \"Tears in Heaven,\" which won three Grammy Awards in 1993."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/29/Rush_soundtrack.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rush_(1991_soundtrack)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1e/%22Starman%22_emblem_%28Rush_%222112%22_album%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1e/%22Starman%22_emblem_%28Rush_%222112%22_album%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The \"starman\" logo, created by Hugh Syme, first appeared on the back cover of 2112. The logo became a staple of Rush's early live shows, and was featured on the cover of their double live album All the World's a Stage (1976).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%22Starman%22_emblem_(Rush_%222112%22_album).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Oberheim_OBX.jpg/500px-Oberheim_OBX.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Oberheim_OBX.jpg/500px-Oberheim_OBX.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Oberheim OB-X synthesizer, as used by Geddy Lee on the albums Moving Pictures and Signals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOberheim_OBX.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg/500px-Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg/1280px-Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg/500px-Simmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neil Peart began incorporating Simmons Electronic Drums, beginning with Grace Under Pressure (1984).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASimmons_SDS5_Electric_Drum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rush-in-concert.jpg/500px-Rush-in-concert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rush-in-concert.jpg/500px-Rush-in-concert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rush performing in September 2004","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARush-in-concert.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-TaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_%28174_of_281%29_%2852334835950%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alex Lifeson, guitarist and co-founder of Rush, pictured in 2022","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATaylorHawkTributeWemb030922_(174_of_281)_(52334835950)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rush_star_on_Walk_of_Fame.jpg/500px-Rush_star_on_Walk_of_Fame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm